real time. Much, much faster than&nbsp;
than real time. My goodness, so good!

00:02:39.554 --> 00:02:45.134
And here comes the punchline. Hold on to&nbsp;
your papers, because we should likely not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.134 --> 00:02:51.494
call this technique a NERF variant, because&nbsp;
this does not use neural networks. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.494 --> 00:02:56.054
this is a good old handcrafted&nbsp;
computer graphics technique. Wow.

00:02:56.054 --> 00:03:02.654
So how does all this wizardry work? We take&nbsp;
out the super capable neural networks, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.654 --> 00:03:10.454
replace them with a little human ingenuity, and&nbsp;
it gets better? How? Well, this has two key ideas.

00:03:10.454 --> 00:03:18.614
Now, we have a 3D world at hand which has to be&nbsp;
represented on our 2D screen. Now the Gaussian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.614 --> 00:03:25.094
part means that objects in this scene will&nbsp;
be represented as a sum of many little lumps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.094 --> 00:03:32.294
It is a bit like throwing a small pebble into&nbsp;
water. The pebble might be a small little point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.294 --> 00:03:40.394
but the waves it creates spread out around this&nbsp;
point. Sounds good, right? Well, not so fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.394 --> 00:03:47.534
Unfortunately, in the 3D space, many of these&nbsp;
waves overlap and it is unclear how exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:55.274
they can be transferred onto our 2D screens. And&nbsp;
splatting is a way of throwing these little waves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.274 --> 00:04:02.174
from the 3D virtual world at our 2D screen and&nbsp;
computing what they should really look like. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.174 --> 00:04:08.474
an additional advantage, these waves concentrate&nbsp;
around solid objects, and thus, this technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.474 --> 00:04:15.074
does not have to waste time computing parts of&nbsp;
the scene with lots of empty space. Loving it!

00:04:15.074 --> 00:04:21.674
Idea number two, typically NERF algorithms&nbsp;
are interested in going through every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.674 --> 00:04:28.934
pixel on your screen, and instead, this one is&nbsp;
interested not in pixels, but in the primitives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:36.134
objects in the scene. Now, the concept is not new,&nbsp;
it has been used in computer graphics for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.134 --> 00:04:40.934
but how it is applied to this problem&nbsp;
is the cool and interesting part.

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:45.794
All in all, this is a simple&nbsp;
algorithm, it is really fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.794 --> 00:04:50.594
and we don’t have to sacrifice&nbsp;
quality. I mean, look at that!

